## Architecture & Technology Stack

The platform will use a modern, scalable microservices architecture.

### 1. Frontend (Command Dashboard & Digital Twin)
*   **Framework:** Next.js (React) with TypeScript.
*   **Styling:** Tailwind CSS for a modern, dark-mode, high-contrast operational UI.
*   **2D Mapping / Basic GIS:** Mapbox GL JS (or MapLibre GL JS for open-source compliance).
*   **3D Digital Twin:** CesiumJS (for rendering 3D terrain, buildings, and volumetric disaster overlays).
*   **State Management:** Zustand or React Context for real-time state updates.

### 2. Backend (Data Ingestion & APIs)
*   **Framework:** FastAPI (Python) - highly performant, excellent for AI integration and async data pipelines.
*   **Real-time Comms:** WebSockets (via FastAPI) to push live updates to the Command Dashboard.
*   **Task Queue:** Celery with Redis (for handling background tasks like satellite image processing or Twitter scraping).

### 3. Database (Geospatial & Relational)
*   **Primary DB:** PostgreSQL with the **PostGIS** extension. Crucial for spatial queries (e.g., "Find all hospitals within the flooded polygon").
*   **Caching:** Redis (for fast retrieval of the live incident state).

### 4. AI & Data Fusion Engine
*   **NLP / Info Extraction:** SpaCy or a lightweight HuggingFace LLM (e.g., Llama-3-8B-Instruct or Mistral if resources permit, or GPT-4o-mini via API) to extract entities (Location, Casualties, Road Status) from unstructured text (Media, X).
*   **Clustering / Correlation:** DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise) or simple geo-hashing combined with time-windowing to group reports into single incidents.
*   **Satellite/CV (Simulated for MVP):** OpenCV/Rasterio for processing Sentinel-1 SAR data (water masking). For the hackathon, we will likely pre-process "Before/After" GeoTIFFs to show the result rather than running the heavy compute live.

